<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        ANTI-GOOGLE LOGO — "Null Route"

        An outbound data arrow driven hard to the right and arrested by a solid slab it never
        reaches. The arrow is the telemetry beacon leaving your machine; the slab is the sink —
        the hosts entry, the firewall rule, the disabled service. The gap between the arrowhead
        and the slab is the actual subject: the packet does not bounce and it does not pass. It
        simply stops, and nothing is drawn on the far side.

        It is the only mark considered here that refuses metaphor. It does not draw protection
        the way a shield or padlock does — it draws a flow and a hard stop, which is literally
        what all four enforcement layers do. For an app whose whole pitch is that it will not
        overclaim, a mark that claims only "this specific thing was stopped" is the honest one.

        GEOMETRY IS PIXEL-HINTED. Ink bounds are exactly (4,4,56,56) on the 64x64 viewbox —
        square, with 4 units of padding on every side. Every axis-aligned edge is divisible by 4,
        so at 16px they land on whole pixels (1/4/12/15 across, 1/6/10/13/15 down) with zero
        antialiasing on any straight edge. The same holds at 32, 64 and 256.

        RULES (do not "improve" these away):
          - Accent #FF5A4E on dark surfaces; ink on light. #FF5A4E on white is ~3:1 and fails at
            icon scale.
          - Never mirror it. Mirrored, the arrow enters the slab from the right and reads as
            inbound/import — the opposite message.
          - Never place it in a badge, ring, tile or rounded-square container. That is Google's
            app-icon grammar regardless of what the glyph inside is, and it would also eat the
            4 units of padding the geometry already carries.
          - Never stroke it, rotate it, or use more than two tonal values.
          - Clear space: 8 units at this scale. Minimum size: 16px.
    -->

    <!-- Canonical mark. Use everywhere except the hand-hinted small ICO frames. -->
    <Geometry x:Key="LogoGeometry">F1 M4,24 H16 V12 L42,32 L16,52 V40 H4 Z M48,4 H60 V60 H48 Z</Geometry>

    <!--
        Small-size frame (16px and 32px ICO entries only).

        The only difference is the arrow apex at 40 instead of 42, which opens the arrowhead-to-slab
        gap from 6 to 8 units — exactly 2px at 16x16 and 4px at 32x32, on whole pixel boundaries.
        At apex 42 that gap spans 10.5..12px, i.e. 1.5px straddling a boundary, and antialiases to
        a grey smear on both flanks. The gap carries the entire meaning of the mark, so in the
        smallest frame it has to be the crispest thing in it, not the softest.
    -->
    <Geometry x:Key="LogoGeometryIcon">F1 M4,24 H16 V12 L40,32 L16,52 V40 H4 Z M48,4 H60 V60 H48 Z</Geometry>

    <!--
        Full-bleed variant for the title bar and any instance at 48px or larger.

        The slab runs off the top and bottom of the canvas. This is what separates the mark from
        the media "skip to end" glyph, which pairs a triangle with a bar of equal or lesser height:
        here the slab exceeds the 40-tall arrowhead by a wide margin and leaves the frame entirely.
        A bar that leaves the frame reads as a wall or a system boundary; a bar contained inside it
        reads as a UI glyph.
    -->
    <Geometry x:Key="LogoGeometryBleed">F1 M4,24 H16 V12 L42,32 L16,52 V40 H4 Z M48,0 H60 V64 H48 Z</Geometry>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LogoBrush" Color="#FF5A4E" />

    <!--
        Deliberately NOT defined: LogoPlateBrush.

        IconGen will draw a rounded plate behind the mark if this key exists. It must not — a
        rounded-square coloured tile is Material / Google app-icon grammar, and it would also
        swallow the padding the geometry already carries. The .ico ships transparent.
    -->

</ResourceDictionary>
